---
source: tests/help_snapshots.rs
---
Show which instrumented code of a run it reached, by file, function or location.
Use it to find code that the run never reached, and the moment where it first
reached a location. Runs created before release 67.6 have no coverage.

The output has a section per module, sorted by module ID. Its header gives the
module ID, its name and language, and its coverage. COVERAGE is the share of the
instrumented locations that the run reached, then the reached and total counts:
`66.7%  2/3`.

By default, each module has one row per file: FILE and COVERAGE.

With --by `function`, each file has a header with its coverage, then one row per
function, in source order: FUNCTION and COVERAGE. A function at 0% is one that
the run never entered.

With --by `location`, each file has a header, then one row per location, in
source order: INPUT_HASH, VTIME and LOCATION. LOCATION is the function, line and
column. INPUT_HASH and VTIME give the moment where the run first reached the
location, and are blank for a location that it has not reached. Pass them as the
input_hash and vtime of `runs logs` to read the logs up to that moment.

Several locations can share a function, line and column, such as the copies of a
generic function in native code, or the two outcomes of a branch in Python. Each
is its own row.

Use --module to show only the locations of a module. Give it more than once for
more modules. `runs modules` lists the module IDs. While the run is in progress,
the output is the coverage up to now.

VTIME is cut to five decimals, which still names a moment at or before the first
reach. With --detail, it prints whole. On a terminal, file names are shortened
at the start, and functions and locations at the end. After the output, a status
line on stderr counts the locations, and gives the share reached and the run's
status.

With --json, every location prints as the JSON object that the API sends, on its
own line, with the vtime as a JSON number: its `module_id`, `address`, `file`,
`function`, and its begin and end lines and columns. A reached location has
`first_reached`, with the `moment` and `run_seconds`, the time in seconds from
the start of the run. So
`snouty runs coverage <run_id> --json > coverage.ndjson` downloads the coverage.

The run must be ready or complete. If it is not, the command waits until it is
ready, and says that it waits.

Examples:
  snouty runs coverage <run_id>
  snouty runs coverage <run_id> --by function --module <module_id>
  snouty runs coverage <run_id> --by location --module <module_id>

Usage: snouty runs coverage [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>
          Run ID

Options:
      --module <MODULE_ID>
          Only list the locations of this module (repeatable)

      --by <BY>
          What each row is: a file, a function or a location [default: file]

          Possible values:
          - file:     One row per file, under a header per module
          - function: One row per function, under a header per module and per
            file
          - location: One row per location, under a header per module and per
            file

  -d, --detail
          Print each vtime whole

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

      --json
          Print NDJSON: one JSON object per line

      --verbose
          Log API requests to stderr (authentication tokens redacted)

      --settings <SETTINGS>
          Path to the snouty settings file (default: ./.snouty.toml; overrides
          SNOUTY_SETTINGS_PATH)

      --profile <PROFILE>
          Settings profile to select (overrides ANTITHESIS_PROFILE)
